Attempt 1: accuracy=71.0% syntax=74.0% (n=100)
  Helpers: unconstrained=UnconstrainedStep (token-by-token); delimiter-triggered << entry; constrained=AdaptiveConstrainedStep; explicit close on IsCompletePrefix
  Rationale: Math-step CSD with delimiter-triggered constrained arithmetic spans.
The strategy tracks the usual generation state (`generated`), whether we are
currently inside a `<< ... >>` constrained region, and the current contents
of that region (`currentConstrainedOut`). Inside a constrained span it also
uses the caller-provided `validTokenGroups` as soft preferences for tokens
that are useful in arithmetic expressions, via an adaptive constrained
sampler.
Action choice is driven by three observations: outside a span we generate
freely so the model can write natural language reasoning; when the token
`"<<"` appears we enter constrained mode with an empty arithmetic prefix;
while inside the span, if the parser says the arithmetic prefix is complete
we close with `">>"`, otherwise we sample the next token from the parser-
valid set, softly boosted by `validTokenGroups` when the grammar is narrow.
This state supports valid generation because `currentConstrainedOut` always
mirrors exactly the suffix of `generated` between delimiters, so parser-valid
updates can be appended to both simultaneously. The parser therefore governs
every token written inside `<< >>`, ensuring arithmetic computations are
emitted as valid constrained spans while leaving explanatory text outside the
delimiters unconstrained.

Attempt 2: accuracy=0.0% syntax=55.0% (n=100)
  Helpers: unconstrained=none; delimiter-triggered << entry; constrained=AdaptiveConstrainedStep; explicit close on IsCompletePrefix; rollback=RollbackToBoundary
  Rationale: Revised strategy: reduce malformed arithmetic spans and encourage the model to
actually emit `<< ... >>` computations during math reasoning, while still
keeping the proof obligations simple.
The previous version waited for the base model to emit `"<<"` on its own and
then sampled arithmetic content with only mild group boosting. The trace shows
that this almost never entered constrained mode, so most outputs stayed fully
unconstrained; when constrained mode was entered, some spans wandered into bad
content or were left open. To fix that, this strategy makes two targeted
changes.
First, outside constrained mode it biases generation toward opening a span
once enough ordinary text has been produced and no active span exists. It does
this with a manual unconstrained sampling step: generate logits, strongly
boost `"<<"`, mildly penalize EOS, then sample. This preserves natural text
before the computation while making arithmetic delimiters much more likely to
appear in step-by-step solutions.
Second, inside constrained mode it uses a stricter arithmetic policy. When the
current constrained prefix is complete, it immediately closes the span using
`CloseConstrainedSpan`; it never treats a sampled `">>"` token as ordinary
content. Otherwise it uses `AdaptiveConstrainedStep` with the caller-supplied
groups, and if the constrained prefix looks narrow / dead-end-prone it
additionally falls back to a rollback-to-boundary recovery branch that trims
the constrained suffix to a valid earlier prefix before continuing. Recovery
consumes a step even though it may append no token, which keeps the loop
provable and prevents repeated malformed continuations.
Overall, the design aims to increase syntax rate by opening spans more
reliably and closing them as soon as the parser allows, and to improve answer
accuracy by forcing arithmetic text inside `<< >>` to remain parser-valid.

Attempt 3: accuracy=70.0% syntax=75.0% (n=100)
  Helpers: unconstrained=UnconstrainedStep (token-by-token); delimiter-triggered << entry; constrained=GroupBoostedConstrainedStep; explicit close on IsCompletePrefix
  Rationale: Revised from the 71%-accuracy baseline with one targeted change: keep the
successful delimiter-triggered design, but make constrained spans easier to
finish once they have started.
The failed follow-up attempt showed that aggressively biasing `"<<"` outside
constrained mode was harmful: it opened spans almost immediately, reduced
syntax rate, and often left spans unterminated. So this version returns to
the baseline policy outside constrained mode: plain `UnconstrainedStep` with
no manual opening bias. That preserves the model's ability to write ordinary
step-by-step text and avoids entering `<< ... >>` too early.
Inside constrained mode, the baseline already closed spans immediately when
`parser.IsCompletePrefix(currentConstrainedOut)` held. The main targeted edit
is to strengthen token selection for arithmetic content by using
`GroupBoostedConstrainedStep` instead of the narrower adaptive heuristic.
This always applies the caller-provided arithmetic token-group preferences
before parser masking, which should help the model complete short arithmetic
expressions more decisively and reduce wandering / unterminated spans, while
still guaranteeing every non-EOS token is parser-valid.
As before, `generated` stores the full answer including delimiters, while
`currentConstrainedOut` stores only the active arithmetic contents between
`<<` and `>>`. When outside constrained mode the tracked constrained prefix is
empty; when inside constrained mode it exactly matches the suffix of
`generated` after the opening delimiter. This lets us append parser-valid
arithmetic tokens to both structures in lockstep and then close the span only
when the parser says the arithmetic prefix is complete.

Attempt 5: accuracy=68.0% syntax=82.0% (n=100)
  Helpers: unconstrained=UnconstrainedStep (token-by-token); delimiter-triggered << entry; constrained=AdaptiveConstrainedStep; explicit close on IsCompletePrefix
  Rationale: Revised from the prior attempt with the minimum targeted fix needed for
verification: the only failing expression was `Contains(generated, "<<")`,
because `Contains` expects a string while `generated` is a token sequence
(`Prefix`). To preserve the strategy shape, we replace that test with the
equivalent token-membership check `"<<" !in generated`.
Everything else remains intact: outside constrained mode we mostly use normal
unconstrained generation, but after enough text has been produced and no prior
`<<` token has appeared, we manually bias logits toward opening a constrained
arithmetic span. Inside constrained mode, if the parser says the arithmetic
prefix is complete we close immediately; otherwise we use
`AdaptiveConstrainedStep` and append the sampled parser-valid token.

Attempt 6: accuracy=63.0% syntax=75.0% (n=100)
  Helpers: unconstrained=UnconstrainedStep (token-by-token); delimiter-triggered << entry; constrained=AdaptiveConstrainedStep; explicit close on IsCompletePrefix
  Rationale: Revised from the 71%-accuracy baseline with one targeted behavioral change:
keep the successful delimiter-triggered structure, but make constrained spans
less likely to drift into long or unfinished arithmetic fragments.
The evaluation failures show two main issues: some spans were left open, and
some constrained contents kept growing into malformed partial expressions like
`<< (100 * b` or `<<k * y / 12 / shark!`. Those traces suggest that once a
span opens, always continuing with the same adaptive sampler can over-generate
symbolic text instead of finishing a short arithmetic computation.
This version therefore keeps unconstrained generation exactly as in the strong
baseline, but changes the inside-span policy to be phase-based. Early in a
constrained span we still use `AdaptiveConstrainedStep` with the caller's
token groups, which helps write arithmetic structure. Once the span becomes
moderately long, we switch to plain `ConstrainedStep`, removing the extra
group bias so the parser-valid distribution can finish naturally. If the span
becomes long enough that continued growth is more likely to hurt than help,
we force termination as soon as the parser reports completeness; otherwise we
still continue one token at a time under parser control. This is a small edit
to the best prior strategy, aimed specifically at reducing unterminated and
malformed spans without reintroducing the harmful eager-open bias.
As before, `generated` stores the full output including delimiters, while
`currentConstrainedOut` stores only the active contents between `<<` and `>>`.
Inside constrained mode, parser-valid tokens are appended to both in lockstep,
preserving the suffix relationship; when the constrained prefix is complete,
we close only through `CloseConstrainedSpan`, never by treating `">>"` as an
ordinary sampled token.

